window.docContents[241].resolve({"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___4___-Roots/Lemma-6___4___6___-Root-coefficients-are-all-nonneg-or-all-nonpos/#Chapter6___Lemma6___4___6":{"contents":"Lemma 6.4.6. Let \\alpha be a root, \\alpha = \\sum_{i=1}^n k_i \\alpha_i. Then either k_i \\geq 0 for all i or k_i \\leq 0 for all i.\n\nProof. Assume the contrary, i.e., k_i > 0, k_j < 0. Without loss of generality, we can also assume that k_s = 0 for all s between i and j. We can identify the indices i, j with vertices of the graph \\Gamma:\n\n\n\\begin{array}{ccccccccccccc}\n\\bullet & \\text{---} & \\underset{i}{\\bullet} & \\overset{\\epsilon}{\\text{---}} &\n\\overset{i'}{\\bullet} & \\text{---} & \\bullet & \\text{---} &\n\\underset{j}{\\bullet} & \\text{---} & \\bullet & \\text{---} & \\bullet \\\\\n&&&& \\vert &&&&&&&& \\\\\n&&&& \\bullet &&&&&&&&\n\\end{array}\n\n\nNext, let \\epsilon be the edge connecting i with the next vertex towards j and let i' be the vertex on the other end of \\epsilon. We then let \\Gamma_1, \\Gamma_2 be the graphs obtained from \\Gamma by removing \\epsilon. Since \\Gamma is supposed to be a Dynkin diagram — and therefore has no cycles or loops — both \\Gamma_1 and \\Gamma_2 will be connected graphs which are not connected to each other:\n\n\n\\boxed{\\begin{array}{ccc}\n\\bullet & \\text{---} & \\underset{i}{\\bullet}\n\\end{array}}_{\\Gamma_1}\n\\qquad\n\\boxed{\\begin{array}{ccccccccc}\n\\bullet & \\text{---} & \\bullet & \\text{---} & \\underset{j}{\\bullet} &\n\\text{---} & \\bullet & \\text{---} & \\bullet \\\\\n\\vert &&&&&&&& \\\\\n\\bullet &&&&&&&&\n\\end{array}}_{\\Gamma_2}\n\n\nThen we have i \\in \\Gamma_1, j \\in \\Gamma_2. We define\n\n\n\\beta = \\sum_{m \\in \\Gamma_1} k_m \\alpha_m, \\quad \\gamma = \\sum_{m \\in \\Gamma_2} k_m \\alpha_m.\n\n\nWith this choice we get\n\n\n\\alpha = \\beta + \\gamma.\n\n\nSince k_i > 0, k_j < 0, we know that \\beta \\neq 0, \\gamma \\neq 0 and therefore\n\n\nB(\\beta, \\beta) \\geq 2, \\quad B(\\gamma, \\gamma) \\geq 2.\n\nFurthermore,\n\n\nB(\\beta, \\gamma) = -k_i k_{i'}\n\n\nsince \\Gamma_1, \\Gamma_2 are only connected at \\epsilon. But this has to be a nonnegative number, since k_i > 0 and k_{i'} \\leq 0. This yields\n\n\nB(\\alpha, \\alpha) = B(\\beta + \\gamma, \\beta + \\gamma) = \\underbrace{B(\\beta, \\beta)}_{\\geq 2} + 2 \\underbrace{B(\\beta, \\gamma)}_{\\geq 0} + \\underbrace{B(\\gamma, \\gamma)}_{\\geq 2} \\geq 4.\n\n\nBut this is a contradiction, since \\alpha was assumed to be a root. \\square\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t6. Quiver representations\t6.4. Roots\tLemma 6.4.6: Root coefficients are all nonneg or all nonpos","header":"Lemma 6.4.6: Root coefficients are all nonneg or all nonpos","id":"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___4___-Roots/Lemma-6___4___6___-Root-coefficients-are-all-nonneg-or-all-nonpos/#Chapter6___Lemma6___4___6"},"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___6___-Reflection-functors/Definition-6___6___1___-Sink-and-source/#Chapter6___Definition6___6___1":{"contents":"Definition 6.6.1. Let Q be any quiver. We call a vertex i \\in Q a sink if all edges connected to i point towards i:\n\n\\begin{array}{ccc} \\longrightarrow & \\overset{i}{\\bullet} & \\longleftarrow \\\\ & \\uparrow & \\end{array}.\n\nWe call a vertex i \\in Q a source if all edges connected to i point away from i:\n\n\\begin{array}{ccc} \\longleftarrow & \\overset{i}{\\bullet} & \\longrightarrow \\\\ & \\downarrow & \\end{array}.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t6. Quiver representations\t6.6. Reflection functors\tDefinition 6.6.1: Sink and source","header":"Definition 6.6.1: Sink and source","id":"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___6___-Reflection-functors/Definition-6___6___1___-Sink-and-source/#Chapter6___Definition6___6___1"},"/8___-Homological-algebra/8___1___-Projective-and-injective-modules/Projective-module/#Chapter8___Definition8___1___2":{"contents":"Definition 8.1.2. A module satisfying any of the conditions (i)—(iv) of Theorem 8.1.1 is said to be projective.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t8. Homological algebra\t8.1. Projective and injective modules\tProjective module","header":"Projective module","id":"/8___-Homological-algebra/8___1___-Projective-and-injective-modules/Projective-module/#Chapter8___Definition8___1___2"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___11___-Tensor-products/Section-2___11___-Tensor-products-___-heading-and-introduction/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___11___-Tensor-products--Section-2___11___-Tensor-products-___-heading-and-introduction":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t2. Basic notions of representation theory\t2.11. Tensor products","header":"Section 2.11: Tensor products — heading and introduction","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___11___-Tensor-products/Section-2___11___-Tensor-products-___-heading-and-introduction/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___11___-Tensor-products--Section-2___11___-Tensor-products-___-heading-and-introduction"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___2___-Algebraic-numbers-and-algebraic-integers/Modification-of-vanishing-argument-without-part-_LPAR_a_RPAR_/#Chapter5___Remark5___2___8":{"contents":"Remark 5.2.8. Here is a modification of this argument, which does not use (a). Let N = |G|. For any 0 < j < N coprime to N, show that the map g \\mapsto g^j is a bijection G \\to G. Deduce that \\prod_{g \\neq 1} |\\chi_V(g^j)|^2 = \\beta. Then show that \\beta \\in K := \\mathbb{Q}(\\zeta), \\zeta = e^{2\\pi i/N}, and that it does not change under the automorphism of K given by \\zeta \\mapsto \\zeta^j. Deduce that \\beta is an integer, and derive a contradiction.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t5. Representations of finite groups: Further results\t5.2. Algebraic numbers and algebraic integers\tModification of vanishing argument without part (a)","header":"Modification of vanishing argument without part (a)","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___2___-Algebraic-numbers-and-algebraic-integers/Modification-of-vanishing-argument-without-part-_LPAR_a_RPAR_/#Chapter5___Remark5___2___8"}});
